<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gayatri Mantra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">Rigveda 3.<a href="/years/62">62</a>.10 (Rigvedic period; dates debated)</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as गायत्री मन्त्र, Gāyatrī Mantra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gāyatrī Mantra, also called the Sāvitrī Mantra, is a verse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> (Mandala 3, hymn 62, verse 10) addressed to Savitr, a Vedic solar deity. It is attributed to the sage Viśvāmitra, who is also regarded as the author of the whole third book of the Rigveda.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The mantra is the verse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> 3.62.10, dedicated to Savitr. In recitation it is preceded by the syllable oṃ and the formula bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ, known as the mahāvyāhṛti or great mystical utterance, as the Taittiriya Aranyaka prescribes. The verse is composed in the metre called gāyatrī, which in principle has three lines of eight syllables each. The text preserved in the Samhita is one syllable short in its first line. A proposed historical form of vareṇyaṃ, vareṇiyaṃ, would restore the eight syllables, and in practice reciters may keep seven, prolong the final m, or use the reconstructed word. The word Gāyatrī also names the metre, the goddess of the mantra, and other verses in the same metre addressed to other deities.</p><p>Translators have rendered the verse in many ways. Ralph T. H. Griffith in 1896 gave it as a prayer that the worshipper may attain the excellent glory of the god Savitar so that he may stimulate our prayers. Stephanie W. Jamison and Joel P. Brereton render the same wish as making our own the desirable effulgence of god Savitar, who will rouse forth our insights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The mantra is cited widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts, including the listings of the Śrauta liturgy, the Srauta-sutras, the grhyasutras, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the Harivamsa and the Manusmṛti. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> treat it esoterically. It has a significant role in the upanayana ceremony, which marks the start of Vedic study. Traditionally this verse was given only to a Brahmana, while other verses were used for a kshatriya and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">vaishya</a>. Reciting it is used as a method of atonement, and practitioners hold that it bestows wisdom through the Sun.</p><p>The Buddha knew the mantra. In Majjhima Nikaya 92 he calls the Sāvittī the foremost of metres, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutta-nipata">Sutta Nipata</a> 3.4 he asks a man who claims to be a brahmin for the chant of Sāvitrī, in three lines of four and twenty syllables. Among Balinese Hindus it is the first of seven sections of the Trisandhyā Puja, said three times a day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1827 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Ram Mohan Roy</a> published a dissertation on the mantra that analysed it against various <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, and from 1830 the Brahmos used it in private devotion. In the later 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> reform movements spread the chant beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> class: in 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a> held that a person became a Brahmana through learning, not birth, and gave the mantra to non-Brahmins in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna-mission">Ramakrishna Mission</a>. Its use is now very widespread.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gayatri Mantra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
